Report the runtime-D3 state in /proc/driver/nvidia/gpus/<id>/power

The driver tracks whether a GPU is in use or idle (nv_dynamic_power_t in
nv-priv.h) and decides from it when to suspend the GPU, but publishes none
of it: its log of the transitions is NV_PRINTF(LEVEL_INFO), compiled out of
release builds, and the kernel only sees pm_runtime get/put. Everything
else that could tell a monitor "busy or idle" (nvidia-smi, PCI config space
and link speed) wakes a suspended GPU, and an RM call from a monitor resets
an idle GPU's countdown to suspend. This appends three lines to the power
file, which already takes the RM API and GPU locks, and which did not wake
a suspended GPU when read every 10 ms (RTX 4080 Laptop, 615.78.08):

  Dynamic Power Management:
   State:                     IN_USE | IDLE_INSTANT | IDLE_SUSTAINED | IDLE_INDICATED
   References:                <n>
   Last Idle Check:           idle|busy, <ms> ms ago   (or "none")

State and References are the existing dynamic_power.state and .refcount.
The last idle check is new: every idleness test (the periodic precondition
check, the recheck before indicating idle) goes through
RmCanEnterGcxUnderGpuLock(), so the original is renamed
RmCanEnterGcxUnderGpuLockUnrecorded() and a wrapper of the old name records
its result and time in two new nv_dynamic_power_t fields. That is what
tells graphics work, which submits through user-mode doorbells and holds no
reference, apart from an idle GPU counting down: both read IDLE_INSTANT.

Five edits, so a refresh for a new driver can redo them by hand when this
stops applying (the PKGBUILD applies it with --fuzz=0):
  1. nv_power_info_t gains four fields, in BOTH copies of nv.h
     (kernel-open/common/inc and src/nvidia/arch/nvalloc/unix/include);
     the two halves of the driver must agree on its layout.
  2. nv_dynamic_power_t (nv-priv.h) gains last_idle_check_idle/_ns.
  3. dynamic-power.c: the rename and wrapper above.
  4. rm_get_power_info() fills the new fields under its existing locks.
  5. nv_procfs_read_power() prints them after the existing lines.

--- a/kernel-open/common/inc/nv.h
